{
  "description": "A handler block is a block scope like any other (spec/execution-model.md:367-369), so a name born inside one goes out of scope at the `]` and no code outside can name that binding (spec/execution-model.md:603-605) — `print :tmp` after the firing raises `ol-undefined-var` (spec/error-model.md:102). A handler INVOCATION is a separate, deferred instruction (spec/execution-model.md:657-660), so the scope it runs in is entered per firing, not shared with the scope that registered it. Added because mutation testing measured that making a handler invocation reuse its captured scope instead of entering a fresh one left the whole conformance corpus green: registration-time capture was pinned in several fixtures, invocation-time scoping in none.",
